  <!-- Headline -->
  <h2>What Actually Makes ByteSIM Different</h2>
  <p>
    Most eSIM apps on this site sell data and nothing else. ByteSIM sells real local phone numbers in select countries, including the US, Canada, UK, France, Spain, Australia, Vietnam, South Korea, and Thailand, bundled with plans that add calls, texts, hotspot sharing, and a local IP address on top of data. That's a genuinely different product from a data-only eSIM, and it's the reason we bought one ourselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    ByteSIM's own site is upfront that most of its travel eSIM plans don't require ID verification, but local-number plans do, per local telecom regulation. That distinction matters because it's not a blanket "hand over your passport for any eSIM" policy the way it might read at first; it's specific to actually getting a real number.
  </p>

  <!-- Real first-hand purchase writeup -->
  <h2>What Buying a Spain Number Actually Involved</h2>
  <p>
    We bought ByteSIM's Spain "Mega Plan with Unlimited Calls & EU Roaming" (SKU spain-eSIM-LBES60GB-27): 100GB of Spain data, 15GB of EU roaming data, unlimited calls within Spain, and 500 minutes of EU roaming calls, for 27 days. Listed at $13.90, it came to <strong>$13.20</strong> after ByteSIM's automatic Summer Sale discount, no code needed. The reason we picked this specific plan over a plain data-only one: it comes with a real <strong>+34 Spanish phone number</strong>.
  </p>

  <img class="content-photo"
       src="/assets/bytesim/bytesim_purchase_reciept.png"
       alt="ByteSIM order summary showing the Spain eSIM Mega Plan with unlimited Spain calls and EU roaming, discounted from $13.90 to $13.20 by the automatic Summer Sale"
       width="660" height="559">
  <p class="content-photo-caption">Our order confirmation. Listed at $13.90, the automatic Summer Sale took it to $13.20 with no code entered.</p>

  <p>
    Getting that number meant identity verification: uploading a passport photo and a selfie, standard practice for getting an actual phone number in many countries, not a ByteSIM quirk. After verification, the eSIM didn't activate right away, which had us worried for a minute. We used the "Get Help" button in WhatsApp and had a real person responding within minutes, who explained the plan won't start billing until you actually tap activate, and to wait 2 to 10 minutes after verification before doing so. It still wasn't showing as active after that. What actually resolved it: ByteSIM support checked back in on their own, unprompted, roughly two hours later to ask if it was working yet. It was. That kind of proactive follow-up, from a real person rather than a bot loop, is a better support experience than most eSIM apps we've dealt with.
  </p>

  <img class="content-photo"
       src="/assets/help2.PNG"
